1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,840
Right now, the phone in your pocket

2
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:04,320
can work out where you are too within a few meters.

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:06,240
And here is the strange part.

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:08,680
It never sends a single message to space.

5
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:10,040
It only listens.

6
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:13,000
So how can a device that never talks to a satellite

7
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,040
know exactly where it is standing?

8
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:18,080
The answer is a story about clocks, distance,

9
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:21,360
and a correction that comes straight from Einstein.

10
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:24,320
GPS is a group of about 30 satellites

11
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:25,920
run by the United States.

12
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:29,920
They circle the Earth at roughly 20,200 kilometers up,

13
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:32,760
far higher than the International Space Station.

14
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:35,680
Each one goes around the planet about twice a day.

15
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:39,000
They are spread out so that, from almost anywhere on Earth,

16
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,800
at least four of them are above the horizon at any moment.

17
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:46,640
Every satellite broadcasts a simple message over and over.

18
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:48,080
It says two things.

19
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:50,740
Here is exactly where I am in my orbit.

20
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:53,880
And here is exactly what time it was when I sent this.

21
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:55,040
That is all.

22
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:57,320
The satellite does not know you exist.

23
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,800
Your phone simply catches these messages

24
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:01,600
as they arrive.

25
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:03,360
Now comes the clever part.

26
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:05,640
The signal travels at the speed of light,

27
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:08,800
about 300,000 kilometers every second.

28
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,760
If your phone knows when the message left the satellite,

29
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:14,960
and when it arrived, it can multiply that travel time

30
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:16,280
by the speed of light.

31
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:18,920
The result is the distance to that satellite.

32
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:23,160
For GPS, that trip takes less than a tenth of a second.

33
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:26,400
Knowing your distance from one satellite is not enough.

34
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:29,560
It only tells you that you are somewhere on a giant sphere

35
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:30,960
around that satellite.

36
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:33,480
Picture it as a circle on a flat map.

37
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:35,960
You could be anywhere on that line.

38
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:38,960
Add a second satellite, and you get a second circle.

39
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,680
The two circles cross in only two places.

40
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:45,240
Add a third, and only one point sits on all three circles.

41
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:46,600
That point is you.

42
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,120
This method is called triliteration,

43
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:50,960
and it works because each distance

44
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:54,440
removes a huge number of possible positions.

45
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:55,960
But there is a problem.

46
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:57,640
All of this depends on time,

47
00:01:57,640 --> 00:01:59,640
and the clock in your phone is cheap.

48
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:03,440
Light travels 300 meters in a single microsecond.

49
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:07,440
So if your phone's clock is off by just 1,000th of a second,

50
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:11,680
every distance it calculates is wrong by about 300 kilometers.

51
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:14,120
That would put you in the wrong city.

52
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:15,920
The solution is elegant.

53
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:17,880
Instead of trusting its own clock,

54
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:21,000
the phone treats the clock error as one more unknown.

55
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,120
Its position has three unknowns,

56
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:25,400
east, west, north, south, and height.

57
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:27,600
Add the clock error, and there are four.

58
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:30,120
Four unknowns need four measurements,

59
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:32,840
so the phone listens to at least four satellites

60
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:35,560
and solves for all of them at once.

61
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:38,440
That is why the satellites carry atomic clocks.

62
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:41,000
These clocks count the vibrations of atoms,

63
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,400
which happen at an extremely steady rate.

64
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:45,640
Because the whole system depends on them,

65
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:48,280
GPS has quietly become one of the world's

66
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:50,360
most important time sources.

67
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:52,560
Phone networks, power grids, and banks

68
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:55,840
all use GPS time to stay in sync.

69
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:58,040
And here is where Einstein comes in.

70
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:00,120
Because the satellites move fast,

71
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:04,320
their clocks run slightly slow by about seven microseconds a day.

72
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:06,880
But because they are farther from Earth's gravity,

73
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:11,400
their clocks also run fast by about 45 microseconds a day.

74
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:13,120
Together, the satellite clocks

75
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:15,880
gain about 38 microseconds every day.

76
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:18,960
If engineers ignored this, GPS positions

77
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:21,560
would drift by roughly 10 kilometers a day.

78
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:25,440
So the clocks are adjusted to account for relativity.

79
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:28,480
Even with all of this, GPS is not perfect.

80
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:30,480
The signal slows down slightly,

81
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:32,680
as it passes through the upper atmosphere.

82
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:36,320
In cities, it bounces off tall buildings before reaching you.

83
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:38,600
And if the visible satellites are bunched together

84
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,840
in one part of the sky, small errors get bigger.

85
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:44,360
This is why your location, sometimes,

86
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:47,120
jumps around between skyscrapers.

87
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,080
So every time you open a map,

88
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:51,560
your phone is quietly listening to satellites

89
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:53,720
20,000 kilometers away,

90
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:56,880
timing their signals, turning time into distance,

91
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:00,280
solving four equations, and correcting for Einstein.

92
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:02,120
All of that happens in a moment,

93
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:03,760
and it never says a word back.
